I wasn't on the Mac dev team, but I have programmed for the Mac.

The Mac (and other systems like the Amiga and even MS Windows to some extent)
prefers to use small relocatable segments instead of one monolithic chunk of
code or data --- this allows for garbage collection/memory compaction, and for
unused segments to be loaded only when necessary (thus freeing up the memory
for other programs; especially important since all three are multitasking in
one way or another).  Memory compaction can cause these segments to move
around while the program is running --- where Unix uses pointers, the three
aforementioned systems use "handles", or double-indirect pointers.  The handle
remains constant, but its value (the pointer to the data) can be changed when
the segment the handle points into is not locked. Segment locks are used for
code that is being executed, and for fast (i.e. pseudo-"real time") access by
the pointer instead of the handle; this causes problems with memory
compaction, so it is preferred to leave segments unlocked whenever possible.

What this means that things can move around in memory, not only between
NetHack sessions, but also in the middle of one.

